'left'}}, default None\n8756 |             Which bin edge label to label bucket with. The default is 'left'\n8757 |             for all frequency offsets except for 'ME', 'YE', 'QE', 'BME',\n8758 |             'BA', 'BQE', and 'W' which all have a default of 'right'.\n8759 |         convention : {{'start', 'end', 's', 'e'}}, default 'start'\n8760 |             For `PeriodIndex` only, controls whether to use the start or\n8761 |             end of `rule`.\n8762 | \n8763 |             .. deprecated:: 2.2.0\n8764 |                 Convert PeriodIndex to DatetimeIndex before resampling instead.\n8765 |         on : str, optional\n8766 |             For a DataFrame, column to use instead of index for resampling.\n8767 |             Column must be datetime-like.\n8768 |         level : str or int, optional\n8769 |             For a MultiIndex, level (name or number) to use for\n8770 |             resampling. `level` must be datetime-like.\n8771 |         origin : Timestamp or str, default 'start_day'\n8772 |             The timestamp on which to adjust the grouping. The timezone of origin\n8773 |             must match the timezone of the index.\n8774 |             If string, must be Timestamp convertible or one of the following:\n8775 | \n8776 |             - 'epoch': `origin` is 1970-01-01\n8777 |             - 'start': `origin` is the first value of the timeseries\n8778 |             - 'start_day': `origin` is the first day at midnight of the timeseries\n8779 | \n8780 |             - 'end': `origin` is the last value of the timeseries\n8781 |             - 'end_day': `origin` is the ceiling midnight of the last day\n8782 | \n8783 |             .. versionadded:: 1.3.0\n8784 | \n8785 |             .. note::\n8786 | \n8787 |                 Only takes effect for Tick-frequencies (i.e. fixed frequencies like\n8788 |                 days, hours, and minutes, rather than months or quarters).\n8789 |         offset : Timedelta or str, default is None\n8790 |             An offset timedelta added to the origin.\n8791 | \n8792 |         group_keys : bool, default False\n8793 |             Whether to include the group keys in the result index when using\n8794 |             ``.apply()`` on the resampled object.\n8795 | \n8796 |             .. versionadded:: 1.5.0\n8797 | \n8798 |                 Not specifying ``group_keys`` will retain values-dependent behavior\n8799 |                 from pandas 1.4 and earlier (see :ref:`pandas 1.5.0 Release notes\n8800 |                 <whatsnew_150.enhancements.resample_group_keys>` for examples).\n8801 | \n8802 |             .. versionchanged:: 2.0.0\n8803 | \n8804 |                 ``group_keys`` now defaults to ``False``.\n8805 | \n8806 |         Returns\n8807 |         -------\n8808 |         pandas.api.typing.Resampler\n8809 |             :class:`~pandas.core.Resampler` object.\n8810 | \n8811 |         See Also\n8812 |         --------\n8813 |         Series.resample : Resample a Series.\n8814 |         DataFrame.resample : Resample a DataFrame.\n8815 |         groupby : Group {klass} by mapping, function, label, or list of labels.\n8816 |         asfreq : Reindex a {klass} with the given frequency without grouping.\n8817 | \n8818 |         Notes\n8819 |         -----\n8820 |         See the `user guide\n8821 |         <https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/user_guide/timeseries.html#resampling>`__\n8822 |         for more.\n8823 | \n8824 |         To learn more about the offset strings, please see `this link\n8825 |         <https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/user_guide/timeseries.html#dateoffset-objects>`__.\n8826 | \n8827 |         Examples\n8828 |         --------\n8829 |         Start by creating a series with 9 one minute timestamps.\n8830 | \n8831 |         >>> index = pd.date_range(\"1/1/2000\", periods=9, freq=\"min\")\n8832 |         >>> series = pd.Series(range(9), index=index)\n8833 |         >>> series\n8834 |         2000-01-01 00:00:00    0\n8835 |         2000-01-01 00:01:00    1\n8836 |         2000-01-01 00:02:00    2\n8837 |         2000-01-01 00:03:00    3\n8838 |         2000-01-01 00:04:00    4\n8839 |         2000-01-01 00:05:00    5\n8840 |         2000-01-01 00:06:00    6\n8841 |         2000-01-01 00:07:00    7\n8842 |         2000-01-01 00:08:00    8\n8843 |         Freq: min, dtype: int64\n8844 | \n8845 |         Downsample the series into 3 minute bins and sum the values\n8846 |         of the timestamps falling into a bin.\n8847 | \n8848 |         >>> series.resample(\"3min\").sum()\n8849 |         2000-01-01 00:00:00     3\n8850 |         2000-01-01 00:03:00    12\n8851 |         2000-01-01 00:06:00    21\n8852 |         Freq: 3min, dtype: int64\n8853 | \n8854 |         Downsample the series into 3 minute bins as above, but label each\n8855 |         bin using the right edge instead of the left. Please note that the\n8856 |         value in the bucket used as the label is not included in the bucket,\n8857 |         which it labels. For example, in the original series the\n8858 |         bucket ``2000-01-01 00:03:00`` contains the value 3, but the summed\n8859 |         value in the resampled bucket with the label ``2000-01-01 00:03:00``\n8860 |         does not include 3 (if it did, the summed value would be 6, not 3).\n8861 | \n8862 |         >>> series.resample(\"3min\", label=\"right\").sum()\n8863 |         2000-01-01 00:03:00     3\n8864 |         2000-01-01 00:06:00    12\n8865 |         2000-01-01 00:09:00    21\n8866 |         Freq: 3min, dtype: int64\n8867 | \n8868 |         To include this value close the right side of the bin interval,\n8869 |         as shown below.\n8870 | \n8871 |         >>> series.resample(\"3min\", label=\"right\", closed=\"right\").sum()\n8872 |         2000-01-01 00:00:00     0\n8873 |         2000-01-01 00:03:00     6\n8874 |         2000-01-01 00:06:00    15\n8875 |         2000-01-01 00:09:00    15\n8876 |         Freq: 3min, dtype: int64\n8877 | \n8878 |         Upsample